          %title
          %p
            This entry is about the article
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de/~streicher/sstt.pdf"} T. Streicher - a model of type theory in simplicial sets - a brief introduction to Voevodsky’ s homotopy type theory
          %blockquote
            %p The aim of this note is to describe in an accessible way how simplicial sets organize into a model of Martin-Löf type theory. Moreover, we explain Voevodsky’s Univalence Axiom which holds in this model and implements the idea that isomorphic types are identical
          %p
            The topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sSet
                  %mo =
                  %mo :
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet=:H
            contains a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural-numbers+object"} natural-numbers object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure"} model structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,W,F)
            (…).
          %p
            Families of types are defined to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibrations"} Kan fibrations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            of Kan fibrations has the following properties:
          %ul
            %li
              %p It is closed under composition, pullback along arbitrary morphisms and contains all isomorphisms.
            %li
              %p
                It is closed under
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:A\to I
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:B\to A
                are in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_a(b)
                is in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %h2#identity_on_
            Identity on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\in H
            be an object. Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi r
                        %mi X
                  %mi Id
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi X
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\xrightarrow{r_X}Id(X)\xrightarrow{p_X}X\times X
          %p
            be a factorization of the diagonal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_X : X \xrightarrow{}X\times X
            into an acyclic cofibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r_X\in C\cap W
            followed by a fibration
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_X\in F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_X
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⊢
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y:X\vdash I_X(x,y)
          %p
            Analogous, for a Kan fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to I
            we factor the diagonal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi I
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_f:A\to A\times_I A
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            in this way.
          %p
            Now the problem is that such a factorization is not stable under pullback. To solve this problem we introduce the notion of
            %em type theoretic universe
            \/
            %em Martin-Löf universe.
          %h2#universes_in__and_lifting_of_grothendieck_universes_into_presheaf_toposes
            Universes in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet
            and lifting of Grothendieck universes into presheaf toposes
          %p
            A
            %em
              universe in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet
            is defined to be a Kan fibration
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mover
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_U:\tilde U\to U
          %p
            Such a universe in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet
            may be obtained by lifting a Grothendieck universe
